💻 Step-by-Step Implementation Runbook


Phase 1: Environment Variable Discovery

Run the following discovery pipeline on the aws-client local terminal to programmatically capture target network IDs, runtime security groups, and IP metrics into environment profiles:
bash
# 1. Fetch Default Public VPC network metrics
DEFAULT_VPC_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-vpcs --filters "Name=is-default,Values=true" --query "Vpcs[0].VpcId" --output text --region us-east-1)
DEFAULT_VPC_CIDR=$(aws ec2 describe-vpcs --filters "Name=is-default,Values=true" --query "Vpcs[0].CidrBlock" --output text --region us-east-1)

# 2. Fetch Isolated Private VPC network metrics
PRIVATE_VPC_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-vpcs --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=datacenter-private-vpc" --query "Vpcs[0].VpcId" --output text --region us-east-1)
PRIVATE_VPC_CIDR="10.1.0.0/16"

# 3. Resolve Target Instances, IP endpoints, and Security Group references
PUBLIC_INSTANCE_ID="i-0802eccd4fd2a569f"
PUBLIC_IP=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids $PUBLIC_INSTANCE_ID --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].PublicIpAddress" --output text --region us-east-1)

PRIVATE_INSTANCE_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=vpc-id,Values=$PRIVATE_VPC_ID" "Name=instance-state-name,Values=running" --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].InstanceId" --output text --region us-east-1)
PRIVATE_IP=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids $PRIVATE_INSTANCE_ID --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].PrivateIpAddress" --output text --region us-east-1)
PRIVATE_SG_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids $PRIVATE_INSTANCE_ID --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].SecurityGroups[0].GroupId" --output text --region us-east-1)


Phase 2: Interface Provisioning & Manual Acceptance

Initiate and formally accept the inter-VPC connection link to move the interface from a provisioning status into an active state:
bash
# Accept the peering connection handshake
aws ec2 accept-vpc-peering-connection \
  --vpc-peering-connection-id pcx-0e32e7a7ee3d44440 \
  --region us-east-1


